(window.STEP_DATA = window.STEP_DATA || {})[17] = {"n":17,"set":"B","level":"Intermediate","band":"B1 45% · B2 55%","sec":[{"id":"R","name":"Reading Comprehension","ar":"فهم المقروء","min":70,"from":1,"to":40,"g":[{"t":"Designing smart cities","p":["By 2050, about two thirds of the world's population is expected to live in cities. This rapid growth creates serious challenges: traffic congestion, air pollution, high energy use, pressure on water supplies and rising housing costs. Many governments and urban planners believe that 'smart cities' could offer solutions. But what exactly makes a city smart, and are there any risks?","A smart city uses digital technology and data to improve the quality of life for its residents and to manage resources more efficiently. Sensors placed throughout the city collect information about traffic, air quality, energy consumption, water use and even rubbish bins. This information is sent through wireless networks to central systems, which analyse it and help authorities make decisions, sometimes automatically.","Transport is one of the most important areas. In some cities, traffic lights adjust their timing according to the actual flow of vehicles, reducing waiting times. Apps show passengers exactly when the next bus or train will arrive, encouraging them to use public transport. Smart parking systems guide drivers directly to free spaces, reducing the time spent driving around looking for a place to park, which can be a significant cause of congestion.","Energy and water can also be managed more efficiently. Smart electricity grids balance supply and demand and make it easier to use renewable energy, such as solar power, which varies during the day. Street lights can dim automatically when no one is around. In water systems, sensors can detect leaks in pipes quickly, which is especially important in dry countries where every litre is valuable.","Saudi Arabia is investing heavily in this field. NEOM, a new region being developed in the north-west of the Kingdom, is planned as a model for future cities, with ambitious goals for renewable energy, digital services and reduced car use. Existing cities such as Riyadh are also introducing smart technologies, including new metro systems and digital government services.","However, smart cities also raise important concerns. Collecting large amounts of data about people's movements and habits creates risks to privacy, and systems may be vulnerable to cyberattacks. Critics also warn that technology alone cannot solve social problems such as inequality, and that residents should be involved in planning decisions. In the end, a truly smart city is not just one with advanced technology, but one that uses technology to make life better, fairer and more sustainable for everyone who lives there."],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["the history of city planning","how to reduce traffic accidents","what smart cities are and their benefits and concerns","the population of Saudi Arabia"],"k":2,"ty":"main idea","e":"The passage defines smart cities and discusses benefits and concerns.","h":"المدن الذكية ومزاياها ومخاوفها."},{"n":2,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a challenge of urban growth?","o":["a shortage of schools","traffic congestion","air pollution","rising housing costs"],"k":0,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"Schools are not listed.","h":"المدارس غير مذكورة."},{"n":3,"s":"According to paragraph 2, what do sensors in a smart city do?","o":["replace all city workers","collect information about city services and conditions","build new roads","control people's homes"],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 2 explains their role.","h":"جمع المعلومات."},{"n":4,"s":"How can smart parking systems reduce congestion?","o":["by banning cars from the city","by building larger car parks","by charging higher fees","by guiding drivers directly to free spaces"],"k":3,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Drivers spend less time looking for parking.","h":"توجيه السائقين."},{"n":5,"s":"Why are smart grids helpful for using solar power?","o":["Solar power is the cheapest energy.","Smart grids produce solar power.","Solar power varies during the day, and smart grids balance supply and demand.","Solar panels need sensors."],"k":2,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 4 explains this.","h":"موازنة العرض والطلب."},{"n":6,"s":"Why is leak detection especially important in dry countries?","o":["Pipes are older there.","Every litre of water is valuable.","Water is free there.","Rain damages the pipes."],"k":1,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 4 says so.","h":"كل لتر ثمين."},{"n":7,"s":"The word 'ambitious' in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to","o":["easy and simple","old-fashioned","requiring great effort to achieve","unclear"],"k":2,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"NEOM has goals that need great effort.","h":"طموحة."},{"n":8,"s":"What concern about smart cities is mentioned in the last paragraph?","o":["too many parks","low internet speed","lack of sensors","risks to privacy"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Collecting data creates privacy risks.","h":"الخصوصية."},{"n":9,"s":"According to critics, what can technology NOT solve alone?","o":["traffic light timing","finding free parking spaces","detecting water leaks","social problems such as inequality"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 6 says so.","h":"المشكلات الاجتماعية."},{"n":10,"s":"What is the writer's view of a 'truly smart city'?","o":["one with the most sensors","one with no cars at all","one that uses technology to improve life fairly for everyone","one controlled entirely by computers"],"k":2,"ty":"attitude / tone","e":"The last sentence states this view.","h":"تقنية تحسن الحياة للجميع."}]},{"t":"The history of clocks","p":["Today, we can check the exact time on our phones, watches, computers and even ovens. It is hard to imagine a world where people could only guess the time of day. Yet accurate timekeeping is a relatively recent achievement, and the story of how humans learned to measure time is a story of scientific curiosity and practical need.","The earliest timekeepers relied on the sun. Ancient Egyptians used tall stone pillars called obelisks and later small sundials, which showed the time by the position of a shadow. Sundials were simple and reliable on sunny days, but they were useless at night or when the sky was cloudy. To solve this problem, people in Egypt, Babylon and China developed water clocks, containers from which water flowed at a steady rate. By measuring how much water had flowed out, people could estimate how much time had passed.","Scholars in the Islamic world made important improvements to water clocks. In the twelfth and thirteenth centuries, engineers such as al-Jazari built elaborate clocks with moving figures, sounds and complex mechanisms that showed the hours. Muslim astronomers also needed accurate timekeeping to determine prayer times and to study the movements of the stars, which encouraged the development of instruments such as the astrolabe.","Mechanical clocks appeared in Europe in the late thirteenth century. They used a falling weight to drive a series of gears, controlled by a device called an escapement, which released the energy in small, regular steps. Early mechanical clocks were large, placed in church towers and often lost or gained fifteen minutes a day. A major breakthrough came in 1656, when the Dutch scientist Christiaan Huygens built the first pendulum clock. Because a pendulum swings at a very regular rate, his clock was accurate to within about a minute a day.","Accurate clocks were particularly important at sea. To find their longitude, their east-west position, sailors needed to know the exact time at their home port. In the eighteenth century, the English clockmaker John Harrison spent decades building marine chronometers that kept accurate time despite the movement of ships and changes in temperature. His work saved many lives by helping ships avoid dangerous mistakes in navigation.","In the twentieth century, quartz clocks used the regular vibrations of a crystal to keep time, making cheap and accurate watches available to millions. Today, the most precise timekeepers are atomic clocks, which measure the vibrations of atoms and would lose less than a second in millions of years. These clocks are essential for technologies such as GPS, which depend on extremely precise time signals."],"q":[{"n":11,"s":"What was the main limitation of sundials?","o":["They did not work at night or in cloudy weather.","They were too expensive.","They were very heavy.","They needed water."],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 2 explains this.","h":"لا تعمل ليلًا أو في الغيوم."},{"n":12,"s":"How did water clocks measure time?","o":["by following the position of a shadow","by counting the swings of a pendulum","by using falling weights","by measuring how much water had flowed out"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 2 explains this.","h":"قياس الماء المتدفق."},{"n":13,"s":"Why did Muslim astronomers need accurate timekeeping?","o":["to determine prayer times and study the stars","to sell clocks in Europe","to help sailors at sea","to build church towers"],"k":0,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 3 gives these reasons.","h":"مواقيت الصلاة ودراسة النجوم."},{"n":14,"s":"What was the function of the escapement?","o":["to make the clock louder","to release energy in small, regular steps","to keep the clock warm","to show the date"],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 4 defines it.","h":"إطلاق الطاقة بانتظام."},{"n":15,"s":"Why was Huygens's pendulum clock much more accurate?","o":["It used water instead of weights.","A pendulum swings at a very regular rate.","It was placed in a church tower.","It used atomic vibrations."],"k":1,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 4 explains this.","h":"انتظام التأرجح."},{"n":16,"s":"The word 'longitude' in paragraph 5 refers to","o":["the speed of a ship","the time at sea","the depth of the ocean","a ship's east-west position"],"k":3,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"The passage defines it.","h":"خط الطول."},{"n":17,"s":"Why was John Harrison's work important?","o":["It made clocks cheaper for families.","It led to the invention of GPS.","It helped ships navigate safely.","It improved sundials."],"k":2,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"His chronometers helped ships avoid navigation mistakes.","h":"ساعد على الملاحة الآمنة."},{"n":18,"s":"Which of the following was invented MOST recently?","o":["the pendulum clock","the water clock","the atomic clock","the marine chronometer"],"k":2,"ty":"sequence","e":"Atomic clocks are the latest.","h":"الساعة الذرية."},{"n":19,"s":"Why are atomic clocks essential for GPS?","o":["GPS depends on extremely precise time signals.","GPS satellites use pendulums.","Atomic clocks are cheap.","GPS only works at night."],"k":0,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"The last paragraph explains this.","h":"دقة الإشارات الزمنية."},{"n":20,"s":"How is the passage mainly organized?","o":["by comparing two inventors","as a list of problems and solutions in one city","chronologically, from early timekeepers to modern clocks","as an argument for and against clocks"],"k":2,"ty":"organization","e":"Sun → water → mechanical → pendulum → quartz → atomic.","h":"ترتيب زمني."}]},{"t":"How cacti survive","p":["Cacti are among the most recognizable plants in the world, with their thick green stems and sharp spines. Native mainly to the Americas, they grow in some of the hottest and driest places on Earth, including deserts where rain may fall only a few times a year. Their survival depends on a remarkable set of adaptations that help them collect, store and save water.","The most important adaptation is the cactus's stem. Unlike most plants, which store little water, cacti have thick, fleshy stems that act as water tanks. A large saguaro cactus in the Sonoran Desert can hold hundreds of litres of water after a heavy rain. Many cactus stems are pleated, like an accordion, which allows them to expand when they absorb water and shrink as they use it.","Cacti also lack ordinary leaves. In most plants, leaves are where photosynthesis takes place, but they also lose large amounts of water through tiny openings. In cacti, the leaves have evolved into spines, and photosynthesis happens in the green stem instead. The spines have several functions. They protect the plant from animals looking for water and food, and they create a thin layer of still air around the stem, which reduces water loss. In some species, dense spines also provide shade from the intense sun.","Another key adaptation is a special type of photosynthesis. Most plants open their pores during the day to take in carbon dioxide, but in a hot desert this would cause a lot of water to evaporate. Cacti open their pores at night, when it is cooler, and store the carbon dioxide in the form of an acid. During the day, they close their pores and use the stored carbon dioxide to make food. This process, known as CAM photosynthesis, greatly reduces water loss.","Cactus roots are also well adapted. Many species have shallow roots that spread widely just below the surface. This allows them to absorb water quickly after even a light rain, before it evaporates. Some cacti can grow new roots within hours of rainfall.","Cacti are important to desert ecosystems, providing food and shelter for birds, insects and bats, which in turn pollinate their flowers. However, many species are threatened by habitat loss and illegal collection. Interestingly, some cacti, such as the prickly pear, have been introduced to other dry regions of the world, including parts of the Middle East, where they are grown for their fruit."],"q":[{"n":21,"s":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["the adaptations that help cacti survive in deserts","how to grow cacti at home","the history of the Sonoran Desert","animals that live in deserts"],"k":0,"ty":"main idea","e":"The passage explains cactus adaptations.","h":"تكيفات الصبار."},{"n":22,"s":"Why are many cactus stems pleated?","o":["to make them look attractive","so they can expand and shrink with water","to protect them from animals","to help them grow taller"],"k":1,"ty":"purpose","e":"Paragraph 2 explains this.","h":"للتمدد والانكماش."},{"n":23,"s":"Where does photosynthesis take place in cacti?","o":["in the spines","in the green stem","in the roots","in the flowers"],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 3 states this.","h":"في الساق الخضراء."},{"n":24,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a function of spines?","o":["protecting the plant from animals","reducing water loss","providing shade","storing water"],"k":3,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"Spines do not store water.","h":"تخزين الماء غير مذكور."},{"n":25,"s":"Why do cacti open their pores at night?","o":["to reduce water loss in the heat of the day","to absorb rain","to attract bats","to release acid"],"k":0,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 4 explains CAM photosynthesis.","h":"تقليل فقد الماء."},{"n":26,"s":"The word 'evaporate' in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to","o":["freeze","be absorbed","become salty","turn into gas and escape"],"k":3,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"Water turns into vapour and escapes.","h":"يتبخر."},{"n":27,"s":"What is the advantage of shallow, wide roots?","o":["They reach deep underground water.","They keep the cactus warm.","They stop animals from eating it.","They absorb water quickly after light rain."],"k":3,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 5 explains this.","h":"امتصاص سريع للماء."},{"n":28,"s":"What does 'which' refer to in paragraph 6 ('which in turn pollinate their flowers')?","o":["desert ecosystems","cactus flowers","birds, insects and bats","prickly pears"],"k":2,"ty":"reference","e":"The animals pollinate the flowers.","h":"الطيور والحشرات والخفافيش."},{"n":29,"s":"What can be inferred about the prickly pear?","o":["It can grow in dry regions outside the Americas.","It is the largest cactus.","It has no spines.","It only grows in the Sonoran Desert."],"k":0,"ty":"inference","e":"It has been introduced to the Middle East.","h":"ينمو خارج الأمريكتين."},{"n":30,"s":"Which sentence best summarizes the passage?","o":["Cacti survive through special stems, spines, photosynthesis and roots that save water.","Cacti need large amounts of rain to survive.","Cacti are dangerous plants that should be removed.","Cacti grow only in the Middle East."],"k":0,"ty":"summary / conclusion","e":"It sums up the main adaptations.","h":"ملخص التكيفات."}]},{"t":"Cooperative learning in classrooms","p":["In many traditional classrooms, the teacher stands at the front and explains, while students listen and take notes. Students work mostly on their own, and success often means doing better than classmates. Over the past few decades, however, many educators have adopted a different approach called cooperative learning, in which students work together in small groups to achieve shared goals.","Cooperative learning is not simply putting students into groups and asking them to work together. Researchers such as David and Roger Johnson have identified several key elements that make it effective. The first is positive interdependence: students must feel that they need each other to succeed. The second is individual accountability: each student is responsible for their own learning and contribution, so that one member does not do all the work while others relax. Other elements include face-to-face interaction, the development of social skills and regular reflection on how well the group is working.","Teachers use many cooperative structures. In 'Think-Pair-Share', students first think about a question alone, then discuss it with a partner, and finally share their ideas with the class. In the 'Jigsaw' method, each member of a group studies one part of a topic and then teaches it to the others, so that everyone depends on everyone else. Educator Spencer Kagan has developed dozens of similar structures, such as 'Numbered Heads Together' and 'RallyRobin', which are now used in schools around the world.","Research suggests that cooperative learning can have many benefits. Studies have found improvements in academic achievement, especially when the elements described above are present. Explaining ideas to others helps students understand them more deeply, and hearing different explanations can help those who are struggling. Cooperative learning also develops communication and teamwork skills, which employers increasingly value, and it can improve relationships between students from different backgrounds.","However, cooperative learning also has challenges. Some students prefer to work alone, and shy students may find group work stressful. Without careful planning, stronger students may dominate discussions or do most of the work. Teachers also need time to train students in group skills and to design tasks that require genuine cooperation. Assessment can be difficult, because teachers must find fair ways to evaluate both group and individual work.","Most experts recommend a balanced approach. Cooperative activities work best when they are combined with clear teacher explanations and opportunities for individual practice. When used thoughtfully, they can help create classrooms where students learn not only from the teacher but also from each other."],"q":[{"n":31,"s":"What is cooperative learning?","o":["a method in which the teacher only lectures","a competition between classes","an approach in which students work together in small groups to achieve shared goals","a system of individual homework"],"k":2,"ty":"main idea","e":"Paragraph 1 defines it.","h":"التعلم التعاوني."},{"n":32,"s":"According to paragraph 2, what is 'individual accountability'?","o":["one student doing all the work","each student being responsible for their own learning and contribution","the teacher marking only the group","students choosing their own groups"],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 2 defines it.","h":"المساءلة الفردية."},{"n":33,"s":"The word 'interdependence' in paragraph 2 suggests that students","o":["work completely alone","compete against each other","depend only on the teacher","need each other to succeed"],"k":3,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"Students must feel that they need each other.","h":"الاعتماد المتبادل."},{"n":34,"s":"How does the Jigsaw method work?","o":["Students think alone and then write a test.","Each student learns one part and teaches it to the group.","The teacher explains everything first.","Students compete to finish first."],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 3 describes it.","h":"كل طالب يعلّم جزءًا."},{"n":35,"s":"Why does the writer mention Spencer Kagan?","o":["to criticize cooperative learning","as an example of an educator who developed many cooperative structures","to describe a famous student","to explain how tests are marked"],"k":1,"ty":"purpose","e":"He developed dozens of structures.","h":"مثال على مطوري الاستراتيجيات."},{"n":36,"s":"According to paragraph 4, why does explaining ideas to others help students?","o":["It helps them understand the ideas more deeply.","It makes lessons shorter.","It reduces homework.","It helps them finish first."],"k":0,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 4 states this.","h":"فهم أعمق."},{"n":37,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a challenge of cooperative learning?","o":["Shy students may find it stressful.","It is more expensive than traditional teaching.","Stronger students may dominate.","Assessment can be difficult."],"k":1,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"Cost is not mentioned.","h":"التكلفة غير مذكورة."},{"n":38,"s":"Which statement is supported by the passage?","o":["Cooperative learning is most effective when its key elements are present.","All group work is effective.","Students should never work alone.","Teachers should stop explaining lessons."],"k":0,"ty":"claim / evidence","e":"Studies found improvements especially when the elements are present.","h":"الفعالية مع توفر العناصر."},{"n":39,"s":"What do most experts recommend?","o":["using only cooperative learning","avoiding group work","combining cooperative activities with teacher explanation and individual practice","letting students teach all lessons"],"k":2,"ty":"summary / conclusion","e":"The last paragraph gives this recommendation.","h":"نهج متوازن."},{"n":40,"s":"What is the writer's attitude towards cooperative learning?","o":["completely negative","uninterested","uncritically enthusiastic","positive but realistic about its challenges"],"k":3,"ty":"attitude / tone","e":"Benefits are presented alongside challenges.","h":"إيجابي وواقعي."}]}],"i":"Read each passage and answer the questions that follow. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","iar":"اقرأ كل نص ثم أجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه، واختر الإجابة الصحيحة."},{"id":"S","name":"Sentence Structure","ar":"تركيب الجمل","min":35,"from":41,"to":70,"g":[{"i":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.","err":false,"q":[{"n":41,"s":"I ______ my keys all morning, and I still can't find them.","o":["looked for","have been looking for","am looking for since","look for"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"have been looking for — Duration up to now: present perfect continuous.","h":"المضارع التام المستمر."},{"n":42,"s":"This software ______ by millions of students worldwide.","o":["uses","is using","has use","is used"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"is used — Present passive.","h":"المبني للمجهول."},{"n":43,"s":"You ______ have been more careful. You nearly broke the vase!","o":["must","can't","should","might not"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"should — Criticism of a past action.","h":"should have للوم."},{"n":44,"s":"That's the reason ______ I decided to study medicine.","o":["why","which","where","whose"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"why — the reason why.","h":"the reason why."},{"n":45,"s":"If I ______ born in Japan, I would speak Japanese fluently now.","o":["was","were","would be","had been"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"had been — Mixed conditional: past condition, present result.","h":"شرط مختلط."},{"n":46,"s":"My father is keen on ______ old cars.","o":["restoring","restore","to restore","restored"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"restoring — keen on + -ing.","h":"keen on + -ing."},{"n":47,"s":"We're having our kitchen ______ next week.","o":["redesign","redesigning","redesigned","to redesign"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"redesigned — Causative.","h":"التركيب السببي."},{"n":48,"s":"Riyadh is not ______ big as Cairo.","o":["so much","as","more","than"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"as — not as … as.","h":"not as … as."},{"n":49,"s":"He ______ have known about the meeting. Nobody told him.","o":["must","couldn't","should","might"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"couldn't — Impossible past deduction.","h":"استنتاج النفي."},{"n":50,"s":"If only it ______ so hot today! We could go for a walk.","o":["isn't","won't be","hadn't been","weren't"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"weren't — Present wish: if only + past.","h":"تمنٍّ عن الحاضر."},{"n":51,"s":"By the time the guests arrive, we ______ the cooking.","o":["will have finished","will finish","finish","are finishing"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"will have finished — Future perfect.","h":"المستقبل التام."},{"n":52,"s":"She told me she ______ me the next day.","o":["will call","would call","calls","has called"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"would call — Reported speech: will → would.","h":"الكلام المنقول."},{"n":53,"s":"______ the fact that he was ill, he went to work.","o":["Although","Even","However","Despite"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"Despite — Despite the fact that + clause.","h":"Despite the fact that."},{"n":54,"s":"It was ______ a long journey that we were exhausted.","o":["so","such","very","too"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"such — such + a + adjective + noun.","h":"such a."},{"n":55,"s":"The news ______ shocking.","o":["was","were","are","have been"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"was — news is uncountable and singular.","h":"news مفرد."},{"n":56,"s":"I'm not used to ______ so early in the morning.","o":["get up","got up","getting up","be getting up"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"getting up — be used to + -ing.","h":"be used to + -ing."},{"n":57,"s":"The students, ______ were from different countries, worked well together.","o":["who","that","which","whom"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"who — Non-defining clause for people: who.","h":"who."},{"n":58,"s":"Not until he got home ______ that he had lost his wallet.","o":["he realized","he did realize","did he realize","realized he"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"did he realize — Not until … at the start: inversion.","h":"القلب بعد Not until."},{"n":59,"s":"They agreed ______ the project by the end of the month.","o":["completing","complete","completed","to complete"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"to complete — agree + to + verb.","h":"agree + to."},{"n":60,"s":"Supposing you ______ the lottery, what would you do?","o":["win","will win","won","had won it"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"won — Supposing + past for an unreal situation.","h":"Supposing + الماضي."}]},{"i":"Choose the underlined part (A, B, C or D) that is incorrect.","err":true,"q":[{"n":61,"s":"(A) Despite of the rain, the (B) players (C) continued (D) training.","o":["Despite of","players","continued","training"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"Despite of — despite takes no of.","h":"despite بلا of."},{"n":62,"s":"She (A) has (B) been (C) study English (D) for six years.","o":["has","been","study","for"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"study — Present perfect continuous: has been studying.","h":"has been studying."},{"n":63,"s":"If I (A) had (B) enough money, I (C) would (D) bought a new car.","o":["had","enough","would","bought"],"k":3,"ty":"error identification","e":"bought — would + base verb: would buy.","h":"would مع المصدر."},{"n":64,"s":"The (A) children (B) was (C) excited (D) about the trip.","o":["children","was","excited","about"],"k":1,"ty":"error identification","e":"was — children is plural: were.","h":"children جمع: were."},{"n":65,"s":"He asked me (A) what (B) time the (C) meeting (D) will start.","o":["what","time","meeting","will start"],"k":3,"ty":"error identification","e":"will start — Reported question: would start.","h":"تحويل الزمن: would."},{"n":66,"s":"The (A) informations (B) on the website (C) is not (D) accurate.","o":["informations","on","is","accurate"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"informations — information is uncountable: information.","h":"information لا تُجمع."},{"n":67,"s":"I (A) look forward (B) to meet you (C) at the (D) conference.","o":["look forward","to meet","at","conference"],"k":1,"ty":"error identification","e":"to meet — look forward to + -ing: meeting.","h":"بعد to هنا -ing."},{"n":68,"s":"The car (A) which my brother (B) bought last year (C) has already (D) broke down.","o":["which","bought","has","broke"],"k":3,"ty":"error identification","e":"broke — Present perfect: has broken down.","h":"التصريف الثالث: broken."},{"n":69,"s":"Hardly (A) had he (B) sat down (C) than the phone (D) rang.","o":["had","sat","than","rang"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"than — Hardly … when (not than).","h":"Hardly … when."},{"n":70,"s":"(A) Although his injury, he (B) played (C) the whole (D) match.","o":["Although","played","the","match"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"Although — A noun needs Despite: Despite his injury.","h":"Despite مع الاسم."}]}],"iar":"الأسئلة من ٤١ إلى ٦٠: اختر الكلمة أو العبارة التي تكمل الجملة إكمالًا صحيحًا."},{"id":"L","name":"Listening Comprehension","ar":"فهم المسموع","min":30,"from":71,"to":90,"g":[{"rec":1,"a":"M17-L-01","q":[{"n":71,"s":"What caused the problem?","o":["The driver went to the wrong city.","The restaurant forgot the order.","The app had the man's old address.","The man was not at home."],"k":2,"ty":"reason","e":"'The app must have my old address.'","h":"العنوان القديم في التطبيق."}],"t":"A wrong address on a delivery app","sc":["Situation: A man speaks to a food delivery driver on the phone. (55 words)","Man: Hello, I'm outside building twelve with your order, but nobody is answering the door.","Man: Oh, I'm sorry. I live in building twenty-one. The app must have my old address.","Man: No problem. Building twenty-one is just around the corner. I'll be there in two minutes.","Man: Thank you. I'll update my address in the app straight away."]},{"rec":2,"a":"M17-L-02","q":[{"n":72,"s":"According to the lecturer, what does climate describe?","o":["conditions today","conditions this week","average conditions over a long period","only temperature changes"],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"'Climate describes the average conditions over a long period'.","h":"متوسط الأحوال لمدة طويلة."}],"t":"Asking a lecturer to repeat a point","sc":["Situation: A student raises her hand during a lecture. (69 words)","Woman: Excuse me, Dr. Adel. Could you repeat the last point about the difference between weather and climate? I missed it.","Man: Of course. Weather describes conditions over a short period, such as today or this week. Climate describes the average conditions over a long period, usually thirty years or more.","Woman: So a cold week doesn't mean the climate is getting colder?","Man: Exactly. You need long-term data to talk about climate."]},{"rec":3,"a":"M17-L-03","q":[{"n":73,"s":"What does the guard suggest?","o":["parking on the street","parking behind the hospital and taking the shuttle bus","cancelling the appointment","waiting for a space"],"k":1,"ty":"function","e":"There is an overflow car park with a free shuttle.","h":"الموقف الخلفي والحافلة."}],"t":"A full car park at a hospital","sc":["Situation: A visitor speaks to a hospital security guard. (60 words)","Woman: Excuse me, the car park is full, and I have an appointment in ten minutes.","Man: There's an overflow car park behind the hospital, near the staff entrance. A free shuttle bus runs from there every five minutes.","Woman: Is it far to walk?","Man: About seven minutes, but the shuttle is quicker. Tell the receptionist you were delayed by parking. They'll understand."]},{"rec":4,"a":"M17-L-04","q":[{"n":74,"s":"Why can the man still finish his report?","o":["He saved it online.","His friend wrote it for him.","The deadline was extended.","His laptop was repaired."],"k":0,"ty":"reason","e":"'I saved it online last night.'","h":"حفظه على الإنترنت."}],"t":"A friend's broken laptop","sc":["Situation: Two friends are talking at university. (54 words)","Man: My laptop won't turn on, and my report is due tomorrow.","Man: Did you save your work anywhere else?","Man: Luckily, yes. I saved it online last night. I just need a computer to finish it.","Man: The library computers are free until nine tonight. Or you can use my laptop this evening; I'm not using it."]},{"rec":5,"a":"M17-L-05","q":[{"n":75,"s":"Why does the man want an aisle seat?","o":["He wants to look out of the window.","He wants to sit with his family.","He wants to leave the plane first.","He needs to stretch his leg because of his knee."],"k":3,"ty":"reason","e":"'I have a bad knee, and I need to stretch my leg.'","h":"ركبته."}],"t":"Requesting an aisle seat","sc":["Situation: A passenger is checking in at an airport. (66 words)","Man: Could I have an aisle seat, please? I have a bad knee, and I need to stretch my leg.","Woman: Let me see. The flight is quite full, but there's an aisle seat in row fourteen. It's next to the emergency exit, so there's more leg room.","Man: That's perfect.","Woman: Just to let you know, passengers in emergency exit rows must be able to help in an emergency."]},{"rec":6,"a":"M17-L-06","q":[{"n":76,"s":"Why was the yoga class cancelled?","o":["The studio is being repaired.","Not enough people booked.","The instructor is ill.","The gym is closing early."],"k":2,"ty":"reason","e":"'the instructor is ill today'.","h":"المدربة مريضة."}],"t":"A cancelled gym class","sc":["Situation: A member speaks to a gym receptionist. (56 words)","Woman: Hi, I'm here for the six o'clock yoga class.","Woman: I'm sorry, the instructor is ill today, so the class has been cancelled. We sent a message this afternoon.","Woman: I didn't see it. Is there another class I can join?","Woman: There's a pilates class at seven in Studio B, and there are still a few places available."]},{"rec":7,"a":"M17-L-07","q":[{"n":77,"s":"What gift will they probably buy?","o":["a watch","a travel bag","a book","a plane ticket"],"k":1,"ty":"inference","e":"'Then a good travel bag would be more useful.'","h":"حقيبة سفر."}],"t":"A colleague's farewell gift","sc":["Situation: Two colleagues are discussing a gift. (54 words)","Woman: Ahmed is leaving the company next Thursday after fifteen years. We should buy him a gift.","Man: How about a watch? Everyone could contribute.","Woman: He already has several. I heard he's planning to travel a lot after he retires.","Man: Then a good travel bag would be more useful. I'll collect the money from the team."]},{"rec":8,"a":"M17-L-08","q":[{"n":78,"s":"What must the student do in Section B?","o":["answer all the questions","write on the answer sheet","skip it","choose and answer three of the five questions Longer conversations"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"'In Section B, you choose three out of five.'","h":"يختار ثلاثة من خمسة."}],"t":"An unclear exam instruction","sc":["Situation: A student asks the exam supervisor a question. (61 words)","Man: Excuse me, the instructions say 'Answer three questions from Section B'. Do I also have to answer all the questions in Section A?","Woman: Yes. Section A is compulsory. In Section B, you choose three out of five.","Man: And should I write the answers in this booklet?","Woman: Section A answers go on the answer sheet. Section B answers go in the booklet."]},{"rec":9,"a":"M17-L-09","q":[{"n":79,"s":"Why do the volunteers want current students to lead tours?","o":["Staff are too busy.","Students know the history better.","It is cheaper.","Visitors prefer hearing from students."],"k":3,"ty":"reason","e":"'Visitors usually prefer hearing from students rather than staff.'","h":"الزوار يفضلون الطلاب."},{"n":80,"s":"Why will one volunteer map a special route?","o":["The main gate will be closed.","The tours are too long.","Some visitors may use wheelchairs.","Some buildings are being repaired."],"k":2,"ty":"reason","e":"Tours should avoid stairs for wheelchair users.","h":"مسار لذوي الكراسي."}],"t":"Planning an open day for new students","sc":["Situation: Two student volunteers are planning a university open day. (148 words)","Woman: The open day is in two weeks. We're expecting about five hundred visitors.","Woman: That's a lot. We'll need clear signs everywhere, especially near the main gate.","Woman: Yes. And I think we should offer campus tours every thirty minutes, led by current students.","Woman: Good idea. Visitors usually prefer hearing from students rather than staff.","Woman: What about the departments?","Woman: Each department will have a stand in the main hall with information and short presentations.","Woman: We should also prepare a quiet room for parents, with water and seats. Some of them wait for hours.","Woman: True. And we must remember accessibility. Some visitors may use wheelchairs, so the tours should avoid stairs.","Woman: I'll map an accessible route tomorrow. Can you organize the tour guides?","Woman: Sure. I'll send a message to the volunteers tonight and arrange a short training session next week, so that every guide knows the key facts about the university."]},{"rec":10,"a":"M17-L-10","q":[{"n":81,"s":"What is the students' main recommendation?","o":["encouraging people to use the metro","building more roads","banning cars from the city","raising petrol prices"],"k":0,"ty":"main idea","e":"'I think it should be encouraging people to use the new metro.' — 'Agreed'.","h":"استخدام المترو."},{"n":82,"s":"Why do they suggest flexible working hours?","o":["Traffic would be spread out over the day.","Workers would sleep longer.","Companies would save money.","The metro would be less crowded at night."],"k":0,"ty":"reason","e":"'traffic would be spread out'.","h":"توزيع الحركة."}],"t":"Discussing a group report on traffic","sc":["Situation: Two students are working on a group report. (146 words)","Man: Our report is about traffic congestion in Riyadh. What should our main recommendation be?","Man: I think it should be encouraging people to use the new metro.","Man: Agreed, but people won't use it unless it's easy to reach the stations.","Man: So we could also recommend more buses connecting neighbourhoods to the metro stations.","Man: And safe parking near the stations, so people can drive part of the way and take the metro for the rest.","Man: What about flexible working hours? If companies started at different times, traffic would be spread out.","Man: Good point. Let's include it as a second recommendation.","Man: We also need data. I'll find statistics on journey times from the transport authority's website.","Man: And I'll write the introduction tonight. Let's meet in the library on Sunday afternoon to combine our parts and check that the report flows well from beginning to end.","Man: Good. Two o'clock?","Man: Perfect."]},{"rec":11,"a":"M17-L-11","q":[{"n":83,"s":"What is the main disadvantage of the apartment next to the gate?","o":["It is too far from campus.","It may be noisy because of the restaurant.","It is too small.","It has no bus stop."],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"'it's above a busy restaurant, so it might be noisy at night'.","h":"الإزعاج."},{"n":84,"s":"How do they solve the problem of walking in the summer heat?","o":["by buying a car","by walking at night","by choosing the other apartment","by taking the university bus Talks and lectures"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"There's a university bus stop on that street.","h":"الحافلة الجامعية."}],"t":"Choosing a place to rent near campus","sc":["Situation: Two students are looking for accommodation. (144 words)","Woman: I've found two apartments near campus. One is a fifteen-minute walk, and the other is right next to the university gate.","Woman: The one next to the gate sounds ideal.","Woman: It is convenient, but it's above a busy restaurant, so it might be noisy at night.","Woman: That could be a problem during exams. What about the other one?","Woman: It's in a quiet residential street, and it's bigger. It's also five hundred riyals cheaper per month.","Woman: A fifteen-minute walk isn't bad. It's good exercise.","Woman: But in summer, walking in the heat could be difficult.","Woman: There's a university bus stop on that street, isn't there?","Woman: Yes, you're right. So we could take the bus in summer. Let's choose the quieter one. I'll call the owner this afternoon and ask if we can see it tomorrow.","Woman: Great. Ask him whether the electricity bill is included in the rent."]},{"rec":12,"a":"M17-L-12","q":[{"n":85,"s":"Why does the world appear mostly grey at night?","o":["Cones work better at night.","Rods, which work in dim light, cannot detect colour.","The pupil closes completely.","The lens stops focusing."],"k":1,"ty":"reason","e":"Rods allow vision in dim light but cannot detect colour.","h":"العصي لا تميز الألوان."},{"n":86,"s":"How does the brain produce the colour yellow?","o":["by using only blue cones","by switching off all cones","by using rods only","by combining strong signals from red and green cones"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Red and green cones both stimulated → yellow.","h":"دمج إشارتي الأحمر والأخضر."},{"n":87,"s":"Why is colour blindness more common in men?","o":["The genes for these cones are on the X chromosome.","Men have fewer rods.","Men spend more time outdoors.","Men's eyes are larger."],"k":0,"ty":"reason","e":"The lecturer gives this reason.","h":"الجينات على الكروموسوم X."}],"t":"Lecture: how the human eye sees colour","sc":["Situation: A biology lecturer speaks to students. (241 words)","Lecturer: Good morning. Today we'll look at how the human eye allows us to see colour. Light enters the eye through the cornea and the pupil, and the lens focuses it onto the retina, a thin layer of tissue at the back of the eye. The retina contains millions of light-sensitive cells called photoreceptors. There are two main types: rods and cones. Rods are very sensitive to light and allow us to see in dim conditions, but they cannot detect colour. That's why, at night, the world around us appears mostly in shades of grey. Cones work best in bright light and are responsible for colour vision. Most people have three types of cones. One type is most sensitive to red light, one to green light and one to blue light. Every colour we see is created by the brain combining the signals from these three types. For example, when red and green cones are both strongly stimulated, we see yellow. Some people have a condition called colour blindness, in which one type of cone does not work properly. The most common form makes it difficult to tell the difference between red and green. It is much more common in men than in women, because the genes for these cones are on the X chromosome. Interestingly, many animals see colour differently. Dogs have only two types of cones, while some birds and insects can see ultraviolet light, which is invisible to humans."]},{"rec":13,"a":"M17-L-13","q":[{"n":88,"s":"According to the conservator, what is the main goal of restoration?","o":["to make manuscripts look new","to stop further damage and keep manuscripts stable","to sell manuscripts to collectors","to rewrite faded texts"],"k":1,"ty":"purpose","e":"'Our main goal is not to make them look new, but to stop further damage'.","h":"منع التلف الإضافي."},{"n":89,"s":"Why do conservators use wheat starch glue and Japanese paper?","o":["They can be removed later without harming the original.","They are the cheapest materials.","They make the paper look older.","They are the only materials available."],"k":0,"ty":"reason","e":"Reversibility is central to the work.","h":"قابلة للإزالة."},{"n":90,"s":"Why are manuscripts photographed in high resolution?","o":["to sell copies to visitors","to replace the originals","so researchers can study them without touching the originals","to check the temperature"],"k":2,"ty":"purpose","e":"The last sentence explains this.","h":"دراستها دون لمسها."}],"t":"Talk: how museums restore old manuscripts","sc":["Situation: A museum conservator speaks to a group of visitors. (233 words)","Lecturer: Welcome to the conservation laboratory. In this room, we care for manuscripts that are hundreds of years old, some of them more than a thousand. Our main goal is not to make them look new, but to stop further damage and keep them stable for future generations. Every manuscript is different, so we always begin with a careful examination. We use microscopes and special lights, including ultraviolet light, which can reveal faded writing and earlier repairs. We also test the inks and paper, because some old inks contain iron and can slowly eat through the paper over time. Once we understand the problems, we decide on the treatment. Common problems include tears, missing pieces, mould and damage from insects. To repair tears, we use thin Japanese paper and a special glue made from wheat starch. These materials are strong but gentle, and most importantly, they can be removed later if necessary without harming the original. This principle, called reversibility, is central to our work. We never use ordinary tape, which becomes yellow and sticky and is very difficult to remove. Environment is also essential. The manuscripts are stored at a stable temperature and humidity, away from direct light, because sudden changes can cause paper to expand, shrink and crack. Finally, we photograph every page in high resolution, so that researchers around the world can study the texts online without touching the fragile originals."]}],"i":"You will hear 13 recordings: 8 short conversations (one question each), 3 longer conversations (two questions each) and 2 talks (three questions each). Read the questions before each recording, then choose the best answer.","iar":"ستسمع ثلاثة عشر تسجيلًا: ثماني محادثات قصيرة (سؤال لكل منها)، وثلاث محادثات أطول (سؤالان لكل منها)، وحديثين أو محاضرتين (ثلاثة أسئلة لكل منهما). اقرأ الأسئلة قبل بدء كل تسجيل."},{"id":"C","name":"Composition Analysis","ar":"التحليل الإنشائي","min":20,"from":91,"to":100,"g":[{"q":[{"n":91,"s":"Which sentence is the best topic sentence for the paragraph?","o":["Many people go to the beach in summer.","Sunlight has both benefits and risks for our health.","Vitamins are found in many foods.","Some skin creams are expensive."],"x":["______ It provides vitamin D, which strengthens bones, and it can improve mood. However, too much exposure can damage the skin."],"k":1,"ty":"topic sentence / thesis","e":"The paragraph presents benefits and a risk.","h":"فوائد ومخاطر."},{"n":92,"s":"Which sentence best fills the blank in the paragraph?","o":["Exams are usually held in June.","A simple study plan can make a big difference.","Some students prefer studying at night.","Libraries are busy during exams."],"x":["Many students struggle to manage their time during exam periods. ______ For example, they can divide their revision into short sessions and set clear goals for each day."],"k":1,"ty":"complete the paragraph","e":"For example needs a general statement about planning.","h":"عبارة عامة قبل المثال."},{"n":93,"s":"Every student in the class (A) have (B) received a (C) copy of the (D) timetable.","o":["have","received","copy","timetable"],"k":0,"ty":"error","e":"Every student is singular: has.","h":"Every مفرد."},{"n":94,"s":"Which sentence does NOT belong in the paragraph?","o":["Sentence 2","Sentence 3","Sentence 4","Sentence 5"],"x":["(1) Public transport has many advantages for large cities. (2) It reduces traffic congestion on the roads. (3) It also lowers air pollution and carbon emissions. (4) Many people enjoy driving long distances at weekends. (5) Furthermore, it is often cheaper than using a private car."],"k":2,"ty":"irrelevant sentence","e":"Enjoying long weekend drives does not support the advantages of public transport.","h":"الجملة خارج الفكرة."},{"n":95,"s":"Which sentence is the best topic sentence for the paragraph?","o":["Some ships are very old.","Oil is found under the sea.","Many workers live near ports.","Sea trade plays a vital role in the global economy."],"x":["______ Ships carry goods such as oil, cars and food between continents, and ports employ thousands of workers."],"k":3,"ty":"topic sentence / thesis","e":"The details show the importance of sea trade.","h":"أهمية التجارة البحرية."},{"n":96,"s":"Choose the best order for the sentences.","o":["2 – 4 – 3 – 1","3 – 2 – 4 – 1","2 – 3 – 4 – 1","2 – 3 – 1 – 4"],"x":["(1) After that, the team analysed the results carefully.","(2) The researchers wanted to know whether music helps students concentrate.","(3) First, they divided two hundred students into two groups.","(4) One group studied with music, while the other studied in silence."],"k":2,"ty":"best order","e":"aim → First → groups → After that.","h":"ترتيب منطقي."},{"n":97,"s":"Which sentence best fills the blank in the paragraph?","o":["Social media was invented in the 2000s.","It allows them to reach new customers at very low cost.","Some businesses are very old.","Many people use their phones at night."],"x":["Social media can help small businesses grow. ______ As a result, many owners now spend time each day creating posts and replying to customers."],"k":1,"ty":"complete the paragraph","e":"The benefit leads to 'As a result'.","h":"الفائدة تؤدي إلى النتيجة."},{"n":98,"s":"Which sentence is the best concluding sentence for the paragraph?","o":["Ambulances are painted white.","Some injuries are minor.","Hospitals are open twenty-four hours.","Everyone should consider taking a first aid course."],"x":["Learning first aid can save lives. Simple skills, such as checking breathing, stopping bleeding and placing an injured person in the right position, can make a huge difference before an ambulance arrives. Many first aid courses take only one day. ______"],"k":3,"ty":"concluding sentence","e":"A recommendation that follows from the paragraph.","h":"توصية."},{"n":99,"s":"The scientists (A) who studied the river (B) found that the water (C) were (D) polluted.","o":["who","found","were","polluted"],"k":2,"ty":"error","e":"the water is uncountable and singular: was.","h":"water مفرد."},{"n":100,"s":"Which word or phrase best fills the blank?","o":["Unfortunately","Therefore","Similarly","For instance Teacher Section — Mock 17"],"x":["The hotel was beautiful and close to the beach. ______, the staff were not very helpful."],"k":0,"ty":"transition / connector","e":"A disappointing contrast.","h":"أمر مخيب."}]}],"i":"Read each paragraph or set of sentences and choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","iar":"اقرأ كل فقرة أو مجموعة جمل واختر الإجابة الصحيحة."}]};
